<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hö'elün</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hoelun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hö'elün was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>, later Genghis Khan. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, she was seized by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesugei">Yesügei</a> from her Merkit husband, and after Yesügei's death she raised her children in poverty when their clan abandoned them. She remained an influential adviser to Genghis Khan and adopted several orphans who became Mongol commanders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hoelun.jpg" alt="Hö'elün" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JukoFF · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Hoelun_Monument_to_Genghis_Khan%27s_Mother_at_the_Mother_Hoelun_Memorial_Complex_in_Tsonjin_Boldog_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Most of what is known of Hö'elün comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, a mid-13th-century epic that is very favourable to her; its anonymous author probably had links to her. According to it she was born into the Olkhonud clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khongirad">Onggirat</a> tribe and was married at around fifteen, in about 1160 or 1161, to Chiledu, brother of the Merkit chief. On the journey to his lands the couple were ambushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> out hawking, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesugei">Yesügei</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> clan. The kidnapping, omitted from most official chronicles, left a grudge among the Merkit that later became a blood feud.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesugei">Yesügei</a> already had a wife, usually named Sochigel by historians, and a son, Behter, but Hö'elün became his primary wife. She bore him four sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>, Qasar, Hachiun and Temüge, and a daughter, Temülen. The year of Temüjin's birth is disputed, with 1155, 1162 and 1167 each favoured by different historians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesugei">Yesügei</a> was poisoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> was about eight. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayichiud">Tayichiud</a> faction excluded Hö'elün from the ancestor ceremonies and abandoned her camp. The Secret History says the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> clan followed, though other sources, such as the Persian historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a>, imply that Yesügei's brothers stood by her. When Temüjin and Qasar ambushed and killed Behter, the Secret History has her angrily reprimand them.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borte">Börte</a>, the two women managed the economy of his camp, and he turned to them first for counsel when he split from Jamuqa. After his defeat at Dalan Balzhut in 1187, Hö'elün married Münglig, an old retainer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesugei">Yesügei</a>, in her third and final marriage. In the following years she probably arranged marriages for her youngest children and maintained alliances while Temüjin was away. The Secret History says she raised numerous foundlings, but problems of chronology indicate that the best known, Shigi Qutuqu, was in fact raised by Börte.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the assembly of 1206 Hö'elün was reportedly granted 10,000 followers jointly with Temüge, and felt that this undervalued her work; Münglig, by contrast, received the seat at the khan's right hand. When Münglig's son, the shaman Kokechu, divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> from his brothers, she defended Qasar and Temüge vigorously, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borte">Börte</a> persuaded Genghis that Kokechu must be eliminated. The Secret History claims that she died soon after, worn out by her efforts, an account that some historians, such as Igor de Rachewiltz, have called poetical melodrama. By the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> she had received the posthumous name Empress Xuanyi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hö'elün” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Secret History of the Mongols, Temüjin's father Yesügei had stolen his wife <b>Hö'elün</b> from a Merkit, and in revenge the Merkits abducted Temüjin's bride Börte, who was rescued with help from Toghrul of the Keraite Khanate and Jamukha.</blockquote><a href="/state/merkit-confederation">Merkit confederation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Khamag Mongol. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,105" aria-label="Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7032 544 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Hö'elün"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7482 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7482 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=46,105">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
